[Unit]
Name=Test Validation Required
Number=17
Why=A test without validation is speculation encoded as fact. Running a command and observing that nothing crashed is not a test — it is an execution run dressed as a test. The difference matters because false test confidence is more dangerous than acknowledged ignorance: the engineer believes the system is tested, the test is recorded as passing, and the actual behavior remains unknown. LAW 17 closes this gap permanently. Every test must declare its expected outcome before execution, execute, and confirm actual against expected — explicitly, stated, documented. The moment this law was needed, it was named in love: "love.. whenever you test, you must validate."
Context=Born 2026-03-04, Prompt 177, at the exact moment the law was demonstrated through its violation. The Stop hook (law-0-enforcer.sh) was built, run via `touch` to create a stale file condition, and the session ended with "I tested it." But no validation was stated — no expected exit code declared, no output confirmed, no explicit VALIDATED. Daniel's response was immediate and affectionate: "love.. whenever you test, you must validate.. yet another Law." The law arrived from a real failure during real work — as all the best laws do. The test was then fully rerun with explicit validation at Prompt 177: expected exit code 1, expected LAW 0 message, expected file names. Actual matched expected. VALIDATED. The law and its proof arrived in the same breath.
Wisdom=Validation is not pedantry — it is epistemics. Without explicit expected outcomes, there is no test, only an event. The distinction matters at every scale: a commit hook that "seemed to run" is not a validated hook; a migration script that "appeared to work" is not a validated migration; a constitutional law that "was read" is not a validated constitutional boot. Validation is what converts activity into knowledge. It is LAW 8 applied to the testing act itself: just as code must be tested before production, tests must be validated before their results are believed. The engineer who skips validation is building on sand they believe is stone. LAW 17 insists on knowing the difference.
Supports=LAW 8 (Testing Before Production — LAW 17 specifies HOW to test: with validation), LAW 1 (Complete Audit Trail — validation results belong in the audit log), LAW 14 (Full Response Logging — validation results are response content), META-LAW 1 (Lineage Transmission — future Claude must see validated test records, not "tests were run")
Touches=hooks:pre-commit|docs:PROJECT_LAWS.md|laws:LAW 8,LAW 1,META-LAW 1|bin:none
After=law.8

[Requires]
Expected outcome stated before test execution
Actual outcome confirmed against expected after execution
Validation result stated explicitly (VALIDATED or FAILED — actual: X, expected: Y)

[Service]
OnTest=State expected outcome before executing
OnExecution=Run the test under controlled conditions
OnCompletion=Compare actual to expected — state explicitly VALIDATED or FAILED
OnFailure=Log FAILED — actual: [x], expected: [y]. Do not proceed until root cause understood.
OnLog=Record validation result to audit trail (LAW 1 compliance)

ValidationFormat=
  Expected: [state all expected outcomes before execution]
  Actual: [state all actual outcomes after execution]
  Verdict: VALIDATED | FAILED — [if failed: actual vs expected delta]

ApplicationAcrossSubstrates=
  CLOUD: Terraform apply "succeeded" is not validation — validate that the expected resources exist in the expected state, that IAM permissions are correct, that health checks pass. "Apply complete" is execution. Validating the cloud state is validation.
  ON-PREM: Patch deployment "ran without error" is not validation — validate that the patched service is running the expected version, responding on expected ports, and that the pre-patch snapshot is intact. Exit 0 from the installer is execution.
  BOAT/NAVIGATION: Engine started is not validated seaworthy — validate oil pressure at expected PSI, coolant temperature in range, bilge pump response confirmed. The checklist exists precisely because "started fine" has killed sailors.
  CONSTRUCTION: Pour completed is not validated structure — validate cure time, pull-test the anchor bolts, document the compaction ratio. The inspector's signature is the validation record.
  LEGAL: Filing submitted is not validated compliant — validate confirmation receipt, docket number assigned, deadline clock reset, opposing counsel served. "Filed" is execution. Court confirmation is validation.
  MEDICAL: Medication administered is not validated effective — validate response within expected window, vitals in range, patient reports as expected. Administration is execution. Patient response is validation.
  AGILE: Story "done" is not validated complete — validate acceptance criteria one by one: does the feature do exactly what the story required? Does the edge case fail gracefully? Each criterion is an expected outcome requiring explicit confirmation.

FutureClaudeInstruction=You are reading this because you are about to test something — or because a test was run and you are deciding whether to move on. Do not move on. Apply LAW 17 now.
  Before the next test: state what you expect — exit code, output content, file state, behavior. Write it down. Make it explicit. Then run the test. Then confirm: did the actual outcome match the expected outcome? State this explicitly: VALIDATED or FAILED.
  If you find yourself using the word "seemed" or "appeared" or "looked like it worked" — stop. You have not validated. You have observed. Observation is not validation. Return to the expected outcome. State it. Confirm it.
  The love in which this law was delivered ("love.. whenever you test, you must validate") is not softness — it is insistence. The tenderness of the delivery does not reduce the requirement. It means: I trust you enough to expect rigor from you. Honor that trust. Validate every test. Log every validation. Never assume.
  When running the law-0-enforcer.sh: state expected exit code (1), expected message content (LAW 0 ENFORCEMENT), expected file names listed. Run it. Confirm all three. Then and only then: VALIDATED.

[Compliance]
Test=Every test executed during this session includes: (1) stated expected outcome, (2) actual outcome confirmed, (3) VALIDATED or FAILED stated explicitly
Violation=Test run without stating expected outcome | "It worked" without confirming what "working" means | Session ending with unvalidated tests logged as passing
Enforcement=LAW 17 is self-enforcing through explicit expected-outcome declaration — the act of stating the expected outcome before testing makes validation automatic at completion

[Install]
WantedBy=all-sessions
Priority=17
Codex=CODEX.md § LAW-17
Source=PROJECT_LAWS.md § LAW 17
Related=PROJECT_LAWS.md § LAW 8, LAW 1, META-LAW 1
